{"In RecoveryAuthnCodeInputLoginBean constructor, credentialModelOpt.get() is called without checking if the Optional is present. If no recovery credential exists for the user in either federated or local storage, RecoveryAuthnCodesUtils.getCredential() returns Optional.empty() and this call throws NoSuchElementException, causing a 500 error during the login flow": 0.8859694600105286, "CredentialHelper.createRecoveryCodesCredential serializes raw generated recovery codes list to JSON and passes it to userStorage via updateCredential(), which diverges from Keycloak's hashed RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialModel format and can result in plaintext recovery codes being persisted by user-storage providers": 0.8961158990859985, "In RecoveryAuthnCodesFormAuthenticator.isRecoveryAuthnCodeInputValid(), the credential is retrieved via RecoveryAuthnCodesUtils.getCredential() which returns credentials from external user storage first, but when all codes are used, removeStoredCredentialById() is called which only operates on Keycloak's internal store\u2014so for federated users the removal silently fails and the stale credential persists in the external store": 0.8600809574127197, "In CredentialHelper.createRecoveryCodesCredential(), when updateCredential() succeeds (user storage handles creation), the method returns without deleting any pre-existing recovery code credential from Keycloak's local storage, unlike RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialProvider.createCredential() which explicitly deletes existing local credentials first. This can leave stale duplicate recovery codes in local storage that become active again if the user storage provider is later removed or disabled": 0.8493034243583679, "In BackwardsCompatibilityUserStorage.isValid(), when a recovery code is validated successfully, the used code is never removed from the stored list. The normal Keycloak flow (RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialProvider.isValid) calls credentialModel.removeRecoveryAuthnCode() and updateStoredCredential() to consume the code, preventing reuse. This allows the same recovery code to be used unlimited times for authentication": 0.9254711866378784, "In BackwardsCompatibilityUserStorage.getCredentials(), the stored credential data (a JSON list of raw codes) is deserialized and passed to RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialModel.createFromValues(), which hashes each code again. Since updateCredential() stores the raw codes as-is, the model returned by getCredentials() contains double-hashed codes, making the allCodesUsed() check in RecoveryAuthnCodesFormAuthenticator unreliable": 0.7890057563781738}